"""King Arthur and the Round Table: a VenvLearning retelling for Grade 4. Chapters 13–17."""
from readers.arthur_a import ch

ch("Sir Percival",
   [("innocent", "not knowing about the bad things in the world"), ("temptation", "a strong wish to do something you know is wrong"),
    ("steed", "a horse, especially a knight's horse"), ("faith", "trust and belief, especially in God")],
   ["Sir Percival had grown up deep in a Welsh forest. His mother had lost her husband and her other sons in battle, and she wanted to keep her youngest son safe. So she raised Percival far from castles and knights. He did not even know what a knight was.",
    "One day, when Percival was a boy, five knights in shining armor rode through the forest. Percival had never seen anything so splendid. He thought they must be angels! The knights laughed kindly and told him that they were knights of King Arthur. From that moment, Percival wanted nothing more than to be a knight himself. He left his mother, rode to Camelot on a farm horse, and eventually won a seat at the Round Table.",
    "Percival was not as clever or polished as some of the other knights. People sometimes laughed at how innocent he was. But he had a good, honest heart, and he was very brave. He was one of the knights who set out on the Grail quest.",
    "Early in the quest, Percival visited his aunt, a holy woman who lived alone. She told him something he had not known. “Your mother died of sorrow after you left,” she said gently. Percival wept. His aunt also told him about Galahad. “Galahad is the best knight in the world,” she said. “If you want to find the Grail, you must find him and stay with him.”",
    "Percival rode on, looking for Galahad. His horse was killed in a fight, and he was left alone in the forest. As he sat sadly by the road, a lady appeared, leading a magnificent black steed. “Take this horse,” she said, “and ride where you like.” Percival thanked her and jumped into the saddle.",
    "The horse took off like the wind. It galloped faster than any horse Percival had ever ridden, and it would not stop. It ran for hours, straight toward a wide, rough river. Percival saw that the horse meant to plunge into the deep water, and he was afraid. He made the sign of the cross. At once, the horse shook him off, leaped into the river with a terrible howl, and the water seemed to burn. Percival understood that the horse had been a demon in disguise, trying to drown him.",
    "Percival found himself alone on a wild mountain surrounded by the sea. He prayed all night. More temptations came to him there. A beautiful lady arrived in a ship and offered him food, wine, and a soft place to rest. She told him she would make him rich and happy if he would forget his prayers and do as she said. Percival was tired, and he almost agreed. But just in time, he saw the cross on the handle of his sword, and he remembered his faith. He made the sign of the cross again, and the lady and her ship vanished in smoke.",
    "Percival was ashamed that he had come so close to giving in. He promised himself that he would be stronger.",
    "The next day, a ship covered in white silk sailed up to the shore. An old man in the ship spoke to Percival and comforted him. Percival climbed aboard, and the ship carried him across the sea. He did not know where he was going, but he was no longer afraid. He trusted that he was being led toward the Grail."])

ch("The Death of Dindrane",
   [("sacrifice", "giving up something precious for the sake of others"), ("custom", "a rule or habit followed for a long time"),
    ("selfless", "caring more about others than about yourself"), ("vessel", "a ship or boat; also a cup or bowl")],
   ["While Percival was crossing the sea, Galahad was having his own adventures. He met Sir Bors, a good and honest knight who was Lancelot's cousin. Bors had kept himself pure on the quest. Even when he had to choose between saving his own brother and saving a lady in danger, he chose to save the lady, because she could not protect herself.",
    "One night, a lady knocked at the door where Galahad was staying. Her name was Dindrane, and she was Percival's sister. “Come with me,” she said. “I will show you the greatest adventure any knight has ever seen.”",
    "Dindrane led Galahad to the seashore, where the white ship was waiting. On board were Percival and Bors! The three knights were overjoyed to be together. Dindrane said, “These three knights are the ones who will achieve the Holy Grail.”",
    "The ship carried them to another, even more wonderful vessel. It was the Ship of Solomon, built in ancient times and kept for the knight who would achieve the Grail. On a bed in the ship lay a mysterious sword. Its belt was made of plain, worn cloth. Dindrane took out a new belt woven with gold and silk and with her own long hair. “I cut off my hair to make this belt,” she said, “for I knew this sword would belong to the best knight in the world.” She fastened the sword to Galahad's side.",
    "After many days, the four companions came to a castle. Its knights rode out and stopped them. “Every lady who passes this castle must give a dish full of blood from her arm,” they said. “That is the custom.”",
    "Galahad, Percival, and Bors refused to let anyone harm Dindrane. They fought the castle's knights bravely and drove them back. But when night came, Dindrane asked to hear the reason for the strange custom.",
    "The lady of the castle had been sick for many years with a disease that no doctor could cure. A wise man had told them that she could be healed only by the blood of a pure maiden who was a king's daughter. Many ladies had given blood, but none had been the right one.",
    "Dindrane thought about the sick lady. “If I give my blood, she will be healed,” she said. “I will do it.” Her brother Percival begged her not to, because he knew she might die. But Dindrane was selfless and brave. “It is better that I die than that so many people go on fighting and suffering because of this custom,” she said.",
    "Dindrane gave her blood. The sick lady was healed at once. But Dindrane grew weaker and weaker. Before she died, she asked one thing of her brother. “Do not bury me here,” she said. “Put my body in a boat and let it drift wherever it goes. When you reach the city of Sarras, where the Grail will be found, you will find me there waiting for you.”",
    "Percival did as his sister asked. With tears in their eyes, the three knights placed Dindrane in a small boat covered with black silk, and they watched it drift away across the sea. Her sacrifice had saved a life and ended a cruel custom. The knights would never forget her courage."])

ch("The Achievement of the Grail",
   [("achieve", "to succeed in doing something difficult"), ("chapel", "a small church or a room for prayer"),
    ("heal", "to make well again"), ("glory", "great beauty and brightness; great honor")],
   ["For a time, Sir Lancelot traveled with his son Galahad on the Ship of Solomon. They were happy to be together. But at last a knight came and told Galahad that he must leave his father. The two said a sad goodbye. They knew they would never see each other again in this world.",
    "Lancelot's ship carried him to the castle of Corbenic, where the Grail was kept. He walked through the castle until he came to a closed door. Through it he saw a bright light and heard beautiful singing. The Holy Grail was inside, on a silver table, surrounded by angels. Lancelot stepped into the room. But a fiery wind struck him, and he fell to the floor. For twenty-four days he lay as if asleep.",
    "When Lancelot woke up, he understood. He had been allowed to see the Grail, but only partly. He was a great knight, but his heart was not pure enough to come all the way into its presence. Lancelot was humble about it. He returned to Camelot, grateful for what he had been given.",
    "Meanwhile, Galahad, Percival, and Bors came together again and rode to Corbenic. There they met the wounded king, who had suffered for many years. Since the Dolorous Stroke, his wound had never healed, and his kingdom had been a wasteland.",
    "That night, the Holy Grail appeared to the three knights in the chapel of the castle. Christ himself appeared and gave them Holy Communion from it. Then Galahad took the bleeding spear, the same spear Balin had used in the Dolorous Stroke. He touched the wounded king with it, and the king was healed at last. The wasteland began to bloom again.",
    "The knights were told to take the Grail to the holy city of Sarras, far across the sea. When they reached Sarras, they found the boat carrying Dindrane's body waiting for them, just as she had promised. They buried her there with honor.",
    "The ruler of Sarras threw the three knights into prison, but the Grail fed them every day. When the ruler died a year later, the people of the city chose Galahad to be their king. Galahad ruled kindly. Every morning, he prayed before the Grail.",
    "One year later, on a morning like any other, Galahad went to pray before the Grail with Percival and Bors. This time, the Grail was uncovered, and Galahad saw it clearly at last. He began to tremble with joy. “Now I have seen what I longed to see,” he said. “I do not want to live any longer.” He said goodbye to his friends and asked Bors to give his love to his father, Lancelot. Then Galahad died peacefully, and his friends saw angels carry his soul up to heaven.",
    "At that moment, a hand came down from heaven and took the Grail and the spear away. Since that day, no one on earth has ever seen the Holy Grail again.",
    "Percival became a hermit and died a year later. Bors, the last of the three, sailed home alone to Camelot. When he told King Arthur what had happened, everyone wept for Galahad, Percival, and the many knights who had died on the quest. The Grail had been achieved. But the Round Table would never be the same."])

ch("Sir Lancelot and the Lily Maid of Astolat",
   [("disguise", "clothes or a costume that hide who you are"), ("token", "a small object given as a sign of friendship or love"),
    ("barge", "a flat boat"), ("devoted", "very loving and loyal")],
   ["After the Grail quest, life at Camelot went on, but many seats at the Round Table were empty. King Arthur planned a great tournament at Winchester to bring his knights together again.",
    "Sir Lancelot decided to fight in the tournament in disguise. He wanted to see if he could win without anyone knowing it was the famous Lancelot. On his way to Winchester, he stayed at the castle of Astolat. The old lord of Astolat had two sons and a daughter named Elaine. Elaine was so lovely and gentle that people called her the Lily Maid of Astolat.",
    "From the moment she saw Lancelot, Elaine loved him. She asked him to wear a token for her in the tournament, a red silk sleeve embroidered with pearls. Lancelot had never worn any lady's token before. But he thought that wearing one would make his disguise even better, because no one would expect it of him. So he agreed. He left his own famous shield with Elaine to keep safe, and he borrowed a plain white shield from her brother, Sir Lavaine.",
    "At the tournament, Lancelot and Lavaine fought on the side opposite King Arthur's knights. With Elaine's red sleeve on his helmet, Lancelot defeated knight after knight. No one knew who the knight with the red sleeve was. But Lancelot's own cousins and friends, not knowing they were fighting him, rode against him all at once. One of them struck Lancelot so hard that a spearhead broke off in his side.",
    "Badly wounded, Lancelot rode away from the tournament with Lavaine. He was so weak that he almost died. Lavaine took him to a hermit who knew how to heal wounds.",
    "When Elaine heard that Lancelot was hurt, she rushed to find him. She stayed by his side day and night, nursing him and caring for him until he was well again. She was devoted to him.",
    "When Lancelot was well enough to leave, Elaine told him her feelings. “I love you,” she said. “I want to be your wife.”",
    "Lancelot was very sad. “I thank you with all my heart for your kindness,” he said. “But I cannot marry you, and I cannot love you as you wish. I am sorry.” He offered to give her land and gold so she could marry another knight. But Elaine did not want riches. She wanted only Lancelot.",
    "After Lancelot left, Elaine grew pale and quiet. She would not eat or sleep. Day by day, she grew weaker, until at last she knew she was going to die of sorrow. She asked her father to write a letter for her and to place it in her hand. “When I am dead,” she said, “put me in my finest dress, lay me on a barge covered with black silk, and let the river carry me to Camelot.”",
    "Her family did as she asked. One morning, King Arthur and Queen Guenevere looked out a window and saw a black barge floating down the river. Inside lay the Lily Maid of Astolat, dressed in white and holding a letter. The king read the letter aloud. In it, Elaine said that she had died because she loved Sir Lancelot, and that he had never done anything to harm her. She asked the knights to pray for her soul.",
    "Lancelot wept when he heard. “I am truly sorry for her death,” he said. “She was good and kind. But I could not force myself to love her.” Elaine was buried with great honor, and Lancelot paid for her funeral. Everyone who heard her story felt its sadness: sometimes love cannot be returned, no matter how good both people are."])

ch("The Queen and the Poisoned Apple",
   [("accuse", "to say that someone has done something wrong"), ("innocent", "not guilty"),
    ("champion", "a person who fights for someone else"), ("treachery", "secretly betraying someone")],
   ["One day Queen Guenevere gave a dinner for the knights of the Round Table. She wanted to show that she loved all of them. Among the guests was Sir Gawaine, who loved fruit, especially apples. The queen made sure there was a bowl of apples on the table for him.",
    "But one of the guests, a knight named Sir Pinel, hated Gawaine, because Gawaine had killed one of Pinel's relatives. Secretly, Pinel put poison into some of the apples, hoping Gawaine would eat one.",
    "Instead, another knight, Sir Patrise, picked up a poisoned apple and ate it. He fell down dead at the table.",
    "Everyone jumped up in horror. Because the queen had given the dinner, everyone thought that she must have poisoned the apple. Sir Patrise's cousin, Sir Mador, stood up angrily. “I accuse the queen of murdering my cousin!” he shouted.",
    "In those days, when someone was accused of a terrible crime, the matter could be settled by a trial by combat. The accuser would fight a champion chosen by the person accused. If the accuser won, the accused person would be punished, even if it was the queen. If the champion won, the accused person would be declared innocent.",
    "King Arthur was very upset. As king, he had to be fair to everyone, and he could not fight for his own wife. Lancelot would have defended her, but he was far away from Camelot. The queen asked one knight after another to be her champion, but they all refused. They were afraid the queen really was guilty.",
    "At last Sir Bors agreed to fight for the queen, though he hoped someone better would come. The day of the trial arrived. A great fire was prepared, because if the queen's champion lost, she would be burned.",
    "Just as Sir Bors was about to ride out, an unknown knight on a white horse came galloping onto the field. He carried a strange shield. “Let me fight for the queen,” he said. Sir Bors stepped aside.",
    "The stranger fought Sir Mador. Mador was strong, but the stranger was stronger. At last Mador fell to the ground and gave up. Then the stranger took off his helmet, and everyone cheered. It was Sir Lancelot!",
    "But the full truth was still not known. Then Nimue, the Lady of the Lake, came to Camelot. With her magic, she discovered what had really happened. She told everyone that Sir Pinel had poisoned the apples to kill Gawaine. Pinel had already run away. The queen was completely innocent.",
    "Sir Mador apologized to the queen, and the king and queen forgave him. Everyone thanked Lancelot. The queen was safe, and the treachery had been uncovered. But the knights noticed something: once again, it was Lancelot who had come to the queen's rescue. Some of them began to whisper about it."])
